---
import Lab from '../../layouts/Lab.astro';
import Term from '../../components/Term.astro';
import RuleAnatomy from '../../islands/pipeline/RuleAnatomy';
import PipelineExplorer from '../../islands/pipeline/PipelineExplorer';
import LatencyLab from '../../islands/pipeline/LatencyLab';
import { ORACLE_INTERNAL, confirmed } from '../../content/site';

const sections = [
  { id: 'plain-words', label: 'In plain words' },
  { id: 'rule', label: 'Figure 1: a rule' },
  { id: 'stages', label: 'Figure 2: the pipeline' },
  { id: 'latency', label: 'Figure 3: latency' },
  { id: 'how', label: 'How it works' },
  { id: 'trade-offs', label: 'Decisions and trade-offs' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'Limits and next steps' },
  { id: 'real', label: 'What is real here' },
];
---

<Lab
  slug="pipeline"
  title="Approval rules from plain English"
  context="Oracle, 2023 to 2025"
  summary="An administrator types what they want. Nineteen prompts turn it into approval rules."
  sections={sections}
  facts={[
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Proposed it, designed it, built it and took it to production' },
    { label: 'When', value: 'During 2023 to 2025' },
    { label: 'Team', value: 'Transaction Framework, Oracle HCM' },
    { label: 'Tools', value: "Oracle's internal LLM APIs and Prompt Lab" },
    { label: 'Links', value: `None. ${ORACLE_INTERNAL}` },
  ]}
>
  <section id="plain-words" aria-labelledby="plain-words-h">
    <h2 id="plain-words-h">In plain words</h2>
    <p>
      In HR software, an <Term id="approval-rule">approval rule</Term> decides who must sign off a request and under what
      conditions. Setting these rules up by hand takes many screens and many clicks. This feature lets an administrator
      type the change in plain English. A pipeline of 19 small <Term id="prompt">prompts</Term> splits the request, works
      out each condition and each list of approvers, and returns rules the screen can draw and save.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="figure-section">
    <RuleAnatomy client:visible />
  </section>

  <section class="figure-section">
    <PipelineExplorer client:visible />
  </section>

  <section class="figure-section">
    <LatencyLab client:visible />
  </section>

  <section id="how" aria-labelledby="how-h">
    <h2 id="how-h">How it works</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>The administrator types a request in the Approval Rules screen.</li>
      <li>Split breaks it into add, update and delete parts and names the rule each part targets. Its output is JSON.</li>
      <li>
        For an add or an update, two things run side by side. Condition extracts the condition and Semantic match ties
        its attributes to real ones. Classify picks one of seven <Term id="list-builder">list builders</Term>, and that
        builder's prompt fills in its settings.
      </li>
      <li>For a delete, one prompt finds which list builder to remove and where it sits.</li>
      <li>The results are merged into the existing rules JSON.</li>
      <li>The screen draws the updated rules for the administrator, and saving goes through the existing rules API.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="trade-offs" aria-labelledby="trade-offs-h">
    <h2 id="trade-offs-h">Decisions and trade-offs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>JSON between every step, so each prompt's output can be checked by code before the next step uses it.</li>
      <li>Parallel branches over one long chain, because waiting time grew with every call.</li>
      {confirmed.smallPromptsReason && <li>Many small prompts, each with one job. Each prompt has one job, so each could be tested on its own with the prompt testing tool.</li>}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="limits" aria-labelledby="limits-h">
    <h2 id="limits-h">Limits and what I would do next</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>This page is a rebuild from memory. Prompts, data and the names of internal systems are left out.</li>
      <li>The timings are estimates from two figures measured at the time.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="real" aria-labelledby="real-h">
    <h2 id="real-h">What is real here</h2>
    <p class="real-box">
      The stages, their order, which of them run side by side, the seven list builders and the two timing figures are
      real. The requests, the rule names and the JSON in the inspector were written for this page. No Oracle code, prompt
      or data appears here.
    </p>
  </section>
</Lab>
